.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1200 PM PDT Fri Oct 23 2026
(This evening through Sunday)

Mostly clear sky conditions are expected by this afternoon across
all of the Bay Area and north Central Coast as high pressure over
the eastern Pacific builds and shifts eastward. However, the
presence of the exiting trough will keep conditions slightly below
seasonal averages. As a separate mid/upper level cut-off low moves
into the northern portion of the Intermountain West, we will have a
brief period of moderate offshore winds (locally strong in the
highest peaks) across the North Bay Interior Mountains with
northeasterly gusts to around 30-40 MPH late this evening and into
Saturday morning. Again, the highest peaks have the potential to
exceed 50 MPH. Elsewhere across the Bay Area, offshore winds will be
weaker but still exist in elevations generally above 1,000 feet.
Given the offshore flow aloft over much of the Bay Area, have lower
confidence of fog development again overnight. That said, cannot
rule out fog again over the North Bay Interior Valleys just before
sunrise Saturday morning.

Saturday and Sunday afternoon maximum forecast temperatures will
warm to near to slightly above seasonal averages as high pressure
continues to build over the eastern Pacific. However, minor coastal
flooding looks to return to bayshore locations along the San Pablo
Bay, San Francisco Bay, and Monterey Bay on Saturday and then low-
lying areas along the Pacific Coast Monday during and around times
of peak high tide. This is due to thermal expansion of the warm
waters generated by El Nino and astronomical high tides through at
least the middle of next week.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1200 PM PDT Fri Oct 23 2026
(Sunday night through next Thursday)

Temperatures drop to slightly below normal for much of next week as
a series of troughs impact the Pacific Northwest. Thus, we are not
expecting any precipitation until late in the week and into the
following weekend (October 30th-November 1st). This is the time
frame in which the Climate Prediction Center`s 6-10 and 8-14 Day
Outlooks give the Bay Area and north Central Coast a 50-60%
probability of above normal precipitation which is more typical for
a strong El Nino year as the Pacific jet stream drops farther south
over southern California. Be sure to stay up-to-date with the latest
forecast information from trusted sources.

